26-06-19T16:54:35Z"},"comments":3,"created_at":"2025-10-16T20:22:57Z","updated_at":"2026-03-04T17:23:53Z","closed_at":"2026-03-04T17:23:53Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in godot 4.6.dev [25981be] and later\n\n### System information\n\nNixOS - Godot v4.6.dev \n\n### Issue description\n\nWith the “Insert at current time” toggle enabled (added in [PR #107511](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/107511)), inserting keyframes repeatedly at the same timeline position creates multiple keys with tiny time offsets (like 1.00, 1.01, 1.02…).\n\nThis happens because the insertion logic shifts the key slightly instead of overwriting the existing one. While this prevents data loss, it leads to clutter and isn’t what most users would expect.\n\nIdeally, it should either:\n\n* Overwrite the existing key at that position\nor\n* Skip insertion and show a warning or a message\n\nHere’s a quick demo of the issue:\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/08c9a019-a164-40da-9762-feb9783d3fcf\n\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Create a new AnimationPlayer.\n2. Enable “Insert at current time”.\n3. Move the timeline cursor to a specific time (e.g. 1.0).\n4. Press the insert key multiple times.\n\nYou'll see multiple keyframes stacked very close together.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nN/A","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17489292?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo","html_url":"https://github.com/Repiteo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/following{/other_user}",